   "stem": {
      "text": "Let F be the antiderivative of \\( f(x) = \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(x \\right)} \\) with \\( F(0) = 1 \\). Find the exact value of \\( F\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\)."
   },
   "answer_key": {
      "form": "symbolic",
      "mathjson": [
         "Rational",
         4,
         3
      ]
   },
   "worked_solution": [
      {
         "step": 1,
         "text": "Let \\( u = \\cos{\\left(x \\right)} \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Cos",
            "x"
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 2,
         "text": "Then \\( du = - \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} \\, dx \\), so \\( \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} \\, dx = -du \\).",
         "mathjson": -1
      },
      {
         "step": 3,
         "text": "Since \\( F(\\frac{\\pi}{2}) = F(0) + \\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(x \\right)} \\, dx \\), convert the limits: \\( x = 0 \\) gives \\( u = 1 \\) and \\( x = \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\) gives \\( u = 0 \\), so the integral becomes \\( \\int_{1}^{0} - u^{2} \\, du \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Negate",
            [
               "Power",
               "u",
               2
            ]
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 4,
         "text": "An antiderivative of \\( - u^{2} \\) is \\( - \\frac{u^{3}}{3} \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Negate",
            [
               "Divide",
               [
                  "Power",
                  "u",
                  3
               ],
               3
            ]
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 5,
         "text": "The integral is \\( 0 - \\left(- \\frac{1}{3}\\right) = \\frac{1}{3} \\), so \\( F(\\frac{\\pi}{2}) = 1 + \\frac{1}{3} = \\frac{4}{3} \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Rational",
            4,
            3
         ]
      }
   ],
